        <h2 id="hk-income-tax-rate">Hong Kong Income Tax Rate</h2>
        <p><strong>Hong Kong income tax</strong> is one of the lowest in the world, making it a popular destination for expats and businesses. The city operates a <strong>FY Hong Kong taxation system</strong> with only two main direct taxes: Salaries Tax and Profits Tax.</p>

        <h3 id="main-tax-types">Main Tax Types in Hong Kong</h3>
        <p>Hong Kong imposes the following direct taxes:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Salaries Tax</strong>: On employment income for individuals</li>
          <li><strong>Profits Tax</strong>: On business profits for companies and unincorporated businesses</li>
          <li><strong>Property Tax</strong>: On rental income from Hong Kong properties</li>
          <li><strong>Stamp Duty</strong>: On property transactions and certain legal documents</li>
        </ul>

        <h3 id="territorial-tax-system">Territorial Tax System</h3>
        <p>Hong Kong operates a <strong>territorial tax system in Hong Kong</strong>, meaning only income sourced in Hong Kong is taxable. This makes <strong>hong kong foreign source income exemption</strong> a key feature of the regime. Overseas income is generally exempt under <strong>foreign sourced income exemption hong kong</strong>.</p>

        <h2 id="salaries-tax">Hong Kong Salaries Tax Rate 2026</h2>
        <p><strong>Hk salary tax rate</strong> and <strong>hk salaries tax</strong> are calculated using two methods - progressive rates or the standard rate, whichever is lower.</p>

        <h3>Hong Kong Individual Tax Rate Structure</h3>
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Net Chargeable Income (HKD)</th>
              <th>Tax Rate</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>First HK$50,000</td>
              <td>2%</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>HK$50,001 - HK$100,000</td>
              <td>6%</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>HK$100,001 - HK$150,000</td>
              <td>10%</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>HK$150,001 - HK$200,000</td>
              <td>14%</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Above HK$200,000</td>
              <td>17%</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <h3>Standard Rate vs Progressive Rate</h3>
        <p>Taxpayers can choose between:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Progressive rate</strong>: 2% to 17% on net chargeable income</li>
          <li><strong>Standard rate</strong>: 15% on net income (whichever is lower)</li>
        </ul>
        <p>The <strong>hk personal tax rate</strong> effectively provides tax-free allowance of HK$132,000 plus deductions.</p>

        <h2 id="profits-tax">Hong Kong Corporate Tax Rate</h2>
        <p><strong>Corporate income tax in Hong Kong</strong> uses a two-tier system.</p>

        <h3>Company Tax Rate Hong Kong</h3>
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Profits</th>
              <th>Corporations</th>
              <th>Unincorporated Businesses</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>First HK$2 million</td>
              <td>8.25%</td>
              <td>7.5%</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Above HK$2 million</td>
              <td>16.5%</td>
              <td>15%</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <h3>Profit Tax HK Calculations</h3>
        <p>Only profits <strong>sourced in hong kong</strong> are taxable. Overseas profits are exempt under the territorial system.</p>

        <h2 id="property-tax">Hong Kong Rental Income Tax</h2>
        <p><strong>Rental income tax hong kong</strong> is calculated at 15% on the net assessable value after deductions.</p>

        <h3>Property Tax Rate Hong Kong</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Standard rate</strong>: 15% on net assessable rental value</li>
          <li>Deductions include: irrecoverable rent, government rent, and property management fees</li>
        </ul>

        <h2 id="tax-brackets">Hong Kong Tax Bracket Structure</h2>
        <p><strong>Hong kong income tax brackets</strong> (also called <strong>hk tax slab</strong>) for individual taxpayers:</p>

        <h3>2026 Tax Brackets for Individuals</h3>
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Income Bracket (HKD)</th>
              <th>Tax Rate</th>
              <th>Cumulative Rate</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>0 - 50,000</td>
              <td>2%</td>
              <td>2%</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>50,001 - 100,000</td>
              <td>6%</td>
              <td>4%</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>100,001 - 150,000</td>
              <td>10%</td>
              <td>7%</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>150,001 - 200,000</td>
              <td>14%</td>
              <td>10%</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Above 200,000</td>
              <td>17%</td>
              <td>17%</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <h2 id="deductions-credits">Hong Kong Tax Deductions and Credits</h2>
        <p>Various deductions reduce your <strong>hk taxable income</strong>.</p>

        <h3>Mandatory Provident Fund (MPF) Deductions</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Employee MPF contributions: Up to HK$18,000 annually</li>
          <li>Employer MPF contributions: Also deductible</li>
          <li>Voluntary contributions: May qualify under <strong>loan interest income taxable in hong kong</strong> provisions</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Personal Allowances</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Basic allowance</strong>: HK$132,000 for all individuals</li>
          <li><strong>Married person's allowance</strong>: HK$132,000 (or HK$264,000 if spouse is not assessable)</li>
          <li><strong>Dependent child allowance</strong>: HK$120,000 per child</li>
          <li><strong>Elderly parent allowance</strong>: HK$100,000 per parent</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Tax Credits for Healthcare</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Mandatory healthcare contributions under MPF</li>
          <li>Premiums for approved health insurance may be deductible</li>
        </ul>

        <h2 id="expats">Hong Kong Income Tax for Foreigners</h2>
        <p>Detailed information about <strong>income tax in hong kong for expats</strong> and the <strong>60-day rule</strong>.</p>

        <h3>60-Day Rule for Foreign Income</h3>
        <p>Under <strong>hong kong non resident tax</strong> provisions, foreign-source income may be exempt if:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Taxonpayer is present in Hong Kong for 60 days or less in the year</li>
          <li>Salary from Hong Kong employment is received in the year</li>
          <li>No other Hong Kong income in the preceding 3 years</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Dividend Income and Tax</h3>
        <p>Dividends from Hong Kong companies are generally not subject to withholding tax under <strong>hong kong withholding tax on dividends</strong>.</p>

        <h3>Capital Gains Tax</h3>
        <p><strong>Capital gain tax hong kong</strong> does not exist. Profits from the sale of capital assets are generally not taxable unless determined to be revenue by the IRD.</p>

        <h3>Interest Income Taxability</h3>
        <p><strong>Interest income taxable in hong kong</strong> when sourced from Hong Kong banks or financial institutions. However, foreign interest is generally exempt.</p>

        <h2 id="calculators">Hong Kong Tax Calculators</h2>
        <p>Use our free tools to calculate your tax liability:</p>

