        <p>Official online tax filing through <a href="https://www.ird.gov.hk/en/e-tax/index.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-link hover:underline dark:text-link">eTax Hong Kong</a> portal.</p>

        <h2 id="faq-section">FAQs: Hong Kong Income Tax</h2>
        <div class="space-y-6">
          <div class="rounded-lg border border-hairline bg-canvas p-6 dark:border-overlay-lighter dark:bg-surface">
            <h3 class="font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary">What is the current income tax rate in Hong Kong for individuals?</h3>
            <p class="mt-2 text-body dark:text-muted">Hong Kong individual tax rates range from 2% to 17% under the progressive system, or 15% under the standard rate - whichever is lower. The <strong>hk personal income tax rate 2026</strong> provides significant allowances including HK$132,000 basic allowance.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="rounded-lg border border-hairline bg-canvas p-6 dark:border-overlay-lighter dark:bg-surface">
            <h3 class="font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary">How do I file Hong Kong income tax as an expat?</h3>
            <p class="mt-2 text-body dark:text-muted">Expats earning Hong Kong-sourced income must file through the IRD. Use <strong>online tax hk</strong> at eTax portal. Foreign-sourced income is generally exempt under <strong>foreign source income exemption hong kong</strong>.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="rounded-lg border border-hairline bg-canvas p-6 dark:border-overlay-lighter dark:bg-surface">
            <h3 class="font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary">Is there a capital gains tax in Hong Kong?</h3>
            <p class="mt-2 text-body dark:text-muted">No, <strong>capital gains hong kong</strong> is not generally taxable. Hong Kong maintains no <strong>capital gain tax hong kong</strong>, unlike many other jurisdictions.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="rounded-lg border border-hairline bg-canvas p-6 dark:border-overlay-lighter dark:bg-surface">
            <h3 class="font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary">What is the Hong Kong effective tax rate?</h3>
            <p class="mt-2 text-body dark:text-muted">The <strong>hong kong effective tax rate</strong> depends on income level, deductions, and choice between progressive vs standard rate. Most taxpayers pay 5-15% effective rate after allowances.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="rounded-lg border border-hairline bg-canvas p-6 dark:border-overlay-lighter dark:bg-surface">
            <h3 class="font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary">Can I claim dividend income tax in Hong Kong?</h3>
            <p class="mt-2 text-body dark:text-muted">Dividends from Hong Kong companies are typically exempt from withholding tax. The <strong>dividend income taxable in hong kong</strong> situation applies primarily to distribution of profits from trading businesses.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="rounded-lg border border-hairline bg-canvas p-6 dark:border-overlay-lighter dark:bg-surface">
            <h3 class="font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary">What tax forms do I need for Hong Kong?</h3>
            <p class="mt-2 text-body dark:text-muted">For individuals: Form BIR50 (individual tax return). For companies: Form IR56B (employer returns) and profits tax return. Access forms through <strong>hk tax ird</strong> portal.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <h2 id="tax-year">Hong Kong Tax Year and Deadlines</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Tax year</strong>: April 1 to March 31 (Assessment Year)</li>
          <li><strong>Filing deadline</strong>: April 15 (paper); June 18 (e-filing)</li>
          <li><strong>Provisional tax</strong>: Paid in installments April, July, November</li>
        </ul>

        <h2 id="best-practices">Best Practices for Hong Kong Tax Planning</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Choose progressive vs standard rate annually - always pick the lower amount</li>
          <li>Maximize MPF deductions within legal limits</li>
          <li>Claim all eligible personal allowances before tax return</li>
          <li>Maintain records for 5 years for IRD verification</li>
          <li>File early to avoid late-payment penalties</li>
          <li>Consider tax implications before salary negotiations or investments</li>
        </ul>

